</div><div mt-section-origin="Bookshelves/General_Chemistry/Interactive_Chemistry_(Moore_Zhou_and_Garand)/01:_Unit_One/1.07:_Day_7-_Covalent_Molecular_Substances_Hydrocarbons" class="mt-section" id="section_8"><span id="D7.6_Petroleum_Chemistry"></span><h2 class="editable">D7.6 Petroleum Chemistry</h2>

<p class="lt-chem-371680"><strong>Petroleum</strong> (from Latin, petra: &ldquo;rock&rdquo;, oleum: &ldquo;oil&rdquo;) <em>consists primarily of naturally occurring hydrocarbons</em>, predominantly alkanes and cycloalkanes. The alkane chains can be quite long, and properties such as melting point and boiling point usually vary smoothly and predictably as a function of the number electrons in various alkane molecules (Table 1).</p>

